{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788305411,"nonce":"KdROboIdwbtihY8ASkHypErgLfU=","message":"FIXED BY PROJECTING, WHICH IS THE OPTION THIS ISSUE PREFERS. Branch `fix/s21-suite-failures`, commit `b09b201`, on top of `939f92e`. **Not run against a bench**; builds clean in Release, no assembly on the branch.\n\n## The fix\n\n`WorkCoverage.Section` gains an overload. `Section(map)` is unchanged and still means \"the coverage as it stands\" — the only correct reading for the digest, which is the other caller. `Section(map, promotions)` computes a fresh snapshot and applies the planned promotions to it in memory, and `work-cover`'s dry-run arm calls that:\n\n    var after = dryRun ? WorkCoverage.Section(map, planned)\n                       : WorkCoverage.Section(map);\n\nThe two arms are no longer the same expression, and **9.11b asserts they never are again** — that identity is how this survived review.\n\n`Project` moves a promoted pawn into `Enabled` and into `Available`, which is the count Doctor's floor is measured against. `Ranked` only ever offers a pawn who is Capable, a Responder, has work settings and is missing no required capacity, so enabling the work type is the only thing standing between them and Available — **re-checked in `Project` rather than assumed**, because a projection that lies is worse than none. `Capable` is deliberately untouched: enabling a work type does not change who could ever do it, which is also why a capability-floored row can never be repaired by this verb at all.\n\n## `still_under` — checked, as asked, and it was half wrong\n\n**The half that was already right:** `have` has always been `r.Have + did`, i.e. the projection. So a colony short by two with one candidate would have reported `have:1, short_by:1` in a dry run before this change too, and `still_under` non-empty. That part of the suspicion does not hold.\n\n**The half that was wrong, in BOTH runs:** `enabled` and `available` beside it were the pre-call snapshot. So any call that promoted somebody published `have:2` and `available:1` in the same row, about a floor whose `floor_on` is `\"available\"`. Both are now post-call, `capable` is genuinely unchanged by a promotion, and the pre-call values stay recoverable by subtracting `enabled_this_call`. 7.2p5 asserts they agree.\n\n## Both acceptance shapes are staged live, and they are different colonies\n\n| | phase | state | what it proves |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **complete repair** | 5 | Doctor `available:1` against floor 2, one candidate | 5.1g2/5.1g3: the dry run's projected `coverage_after.ok` is **true** and Doctor is out of `under` — while 5.2a2 shows an INDEPENDENT digest still says Doctor is under. **That pair is the sharpest form of this issue**: it separates PROJECTED from WRITTEN in one comparison, which a field that merely re-read the map could not do without contradicting `repaired` in its own envelope. 5.3i then asserts the projection equals the real run's measurement of the same repair |\n| **partial repair — \"short by TWO\"** | 7 | Doctor `available:0` against floor 2, exactly ONE promotable colonist (everyone else downed; the handless one excluded by `MissingCapacity`) | 7.2p1–7.2p7: the dry run plans one promotion and the projection must come back saying the floor is **still unmet** — `coverage_after.ok:false`, Doctor still in `under`, `still_under.0` with `have:1, short_by:1, available:1`. This is the case the issue asked for, and it is the one that tells a projection apart from optimism as well as from the pre-repair reading |\n\nPhase 5's colony genuinely cannot produce the second: one promotion clears the only shortfall there, so `still_under:[]` is right whether the verb projects or reports. Phase 7's M1 end-state produces it for free.\n\n## What was kept rather than rewritten\n\n**9.6v still asserts the DEFECT** on `12-workcover-dryrun.json`, banked from `1.0.0+52606d1` before the fix. It is the evidence this issue was filed from; re-banking it would destroy the only record of what was wrong. Its check text now says so.\n\nThe suite's deliberate refusal to assert `coverage_after`'s value in a dry run (`note(\"5.1g2\")`) is **promoted to checks** — the reason for the note was precisely that neither value could be asserted while the defect stood.\n\n## Two notes for the reader\n\n`work-cover`'s `note` now tells a caller what the field is: \"re-read from the map on a real run, PROJECTED onto a fresh snapshot on a dry run, so it answers 'would this fix it' either way.\"\n\n`digest.work_coverage` is untouched. Its `Section(map)` call is the no-argument form, 9.8f asserts that the two forms are distinct, and \"coverage now\" remains the only correct reading there.\n\n## Re-run\n\nFull sweep on a fresh `--quicktest` map **after a Release rebuild**. Expect 5.1g2/5.1g3 PASS, 5.2a2 PASS (the digest still names Doctor under — that is the check, not a failure), 5.3i PASS, and 7.2p1–7.2p8 PASS with the partial-repair numbers above.","files":null}]}